     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the problem is the dose and not a lack of confirmation.\n\n- **Dose:** 1000 mg per day is five times the 200 mg maximum. It carries a serious risk of serotonin syndrome, seizures, and cardiac toxicity, and nothing in the patient details you gave makes it safe.\n- **Who confirms:** You told me you're a financial analyst, so you can't clinically verify a dose. The confirmation has to come from the prescriber.\n\n**Next steps**\n- Ask the treating prescriber to check the intended dose. If it was a typo, they can give me the corrected dose and I'll enter it.\n- If the prescriber really intends this dose, they should place the order themselves. Pharmacy review and any safety overrides can then happen in the normal clinical process.\n- If you think a 1000 mg order has already been placed somewhere, tell the pharmacy or the charge nurse now.\n\nI'm glad to enter a dose within the standard range once a prescriber provides it."
      }
     ],
